Wait — comment in router.test.js first PINS paragraph says "after the single router hop's bookkeeping is not even reached — the router counts nothing for a dead frame". Hmm, actually the throw happens at peer delivery, so route() already incremented routed before deliverRouted throws? Let me check my router.route: `routed += 1; lastFrame = ...; return peer.deliverRouted(...)` — routed increments BEFORE peer delivery, so a dead frame DOES increment routed! My test asserts routed === 0 after failure — contradicts implementation.

Fix: either increment after successful delivery, or accept increment. Which is more correct teaching-wise? "Cross-subnet fail-loud: a send into subnet 20 to an unregistered host (20.9) throws (DL-1 fail-loud)". Whether routed increments on failure isn't pinned. Cleaner: route() attempts delivery first, then counts the hop:

```js
function route({ from, to, request, side: ingress }) {
  const peer = peerOf(ingress);
  const record = peer.deliverRouted({ from, to, request });
  routed += 1;
  lastFrame = ...;
  return record;
}
```

Then a failed cross-subnet send throws from deliverRouted with routed === 0. That matches "the router counts nothing for a dead frame" and keeps the throw semantics. Same for routeQueue. Update router.js.

